 "case:timeline.events.tl_quarrel.time": "8.45 pm",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_tape.teaser": "How Lionel’s voice was heard at 10.15.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_tape.text": "The old dictation reel plays through the study door",
 "case:timeline.prompt": "Put Saturday night’s events in the order they really happened. Times you know for certain are shown; reason out the rest.",
 "case:timeline.title": "The evening, reconstructed",
 "case:title": "The Midnight Toast",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_daniel_study.insight": "Daniel lied: he was in the study at 10.45. But by his account his father was already dead — which explains the prints on the bookend and the IOU in the grate.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_daniel_study.response.0": "…All right. All right!",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_daniel_study.response.1": "I went in at a quarter to eleven to apologise. Dutch courage, I suppose. He was slumped over the desk. Dead. I knew it the moment I touched his hand.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_daniel_study.response.2": "That bookend was lying on the floor. I picked it up without thinking — then I saw my IOU sitting on his blotter and… God help me, I threw it on the fire. Then I ran.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_daniel_study.response.3": "I didn’t kill him. He was dead when I got there. I swear it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_daniel_study.title": "Daniel was in the study at 10.45",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_calder.insight": "Eleanor admits embezzling through a sham supplier — but clings to the 10.15 ‘dictation’ as her alibi for murder.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_calder.response.0": "…",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_calder.response.1": "Nineteen years. Nineteen years of his letters and his tempers and his name signed in my hand on a thousand cheques. Do you know what he paid me?",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_calder.response.2": "Yes. I invented Calder & Rowe. I took what I was owed. But I did not kill him. He was alive at a quarter past ten — Hobbs heard him.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_calder.title": "Calder & Rowe was Eleanor’s invention",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_letters.insight": "The last letter Eleanor brought him is signed ‘Lionel Th—’, the nib trailing off the page. He didn’t stop because he was tired. He collapsed while she stood beside him.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_letters.response.0": "That? He — he must have been tired. He waved me away before he’d finished. Mr. Thorne often left things half done.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_letters.response.1": "Her hand has crept to her throat. Lionel Thorne, by every account, never left anything half done in his life.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_letters.title": "Lionel died mid-signature",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_typing.insight": "Eleanor’s office was dark and cold all evening. She now admits she was in the study with Lionel at about twenty to ten — but insists he was alive when she left.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_typing.response.0": "…I see.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_typing.response.1": "Very well. I wasn’t typing. At about twenty to ten I took Mr. Thorne the day’s letters to sign. I didn’t mention it because I knew how it would look.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_typing.response.2": "He was perfectly well when I left him at a quarter to ten. And he was dictating at a quarter past, wasn’t he? Hobbs heard him.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_eleanor_typing.title": "Eleanor wasn’t in her office",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_finch_library.insight": "Finch lied to hide his secret deal. The exchange’s charges put him at the hall telephone from 9.32 to 9.51 — with a clear view of the study corridor.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_finch_library.response.0": "…An impression on a telephone pad. How very thorough of you.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_finch_library.response.1": "Very well. I wasn’t in the library. From half past nine I was on the hall telephone to Rupert Hartley, arranging to sell him my shares — whatever Lionel thought of it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_finch_library.response.2": "Nineteen minutes, the exchange said. Business, not murder.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_finch_library.title": "Finch was on the telephone to Hartley",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_blow.insight": "Lionel wasn’t struck down. He was poisoned with cyanide, and grazed his temple on the bookend as he collapsed.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_blow.response.0": "…Let me think again.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_blow.response.1": "The wound barely bled, did it? A man who’s already dying doesn’t bleed much. And that smell in the room last night — I took it for the brandy. Bitter almonds. Good God. Cyanide.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_blow.response.2": "He didn’t die of that blow; he struck his head as he fell. Cyanide kills within minutes. Lionel was poisoned — and I should have seen it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_blow.title": "Harrow misjudged the cause of death",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_room.insight": "Dr. Harrow spent half an hour in the conservatory with Margaret, not in the drawing room.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_room.response.0": "Ah.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_room.response.1": "Yes. I was in the conservatory with Margaret from half past nine until five to ten. We came back to the drawing room separately.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_room.response.2": "It was foolish, and it was wrong. But it was not murder.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_harrow_room.title": "Harrow was in the conservatory",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_margaret_room.insight": "Margaret wasn’t in the drawing room between half past nine and ten. She was in the conservatory with Dr. Harrow.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_margaret_room.response.0": "…Where did you find that?",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_margaret_room.response.1": "Very well. Felix and I were in the conservatory from about half past nine until nearly ten. We are — fond of each other. We have been for some time.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_margaret_room.response.2": "We came back separately so that no one would notice. We were together the whole time. Ask Felix.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_margaret_room.title": "Margaret was in the conservatory",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.conclusion": "Every Calder & Rowe invoice carries the high, faint ‘e’ of Eleanor’s own typewriter. She invented the firm and paid herself — and Monday’s audit would have found it.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.options.0": "A genuine supplier of brass fittings",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.options.1": "A front for Hartley Precision",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.options.2": "A sham firm, its invoices typed on Eleanor’s typewriter",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.options.3": "Daniel’s bookmaker under another name",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.proofLabels.0": "The invoice",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.proofLabels.1": "The machine that typed it",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.question": "What was Calder & Rowe Supplies?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_calder.title": "Calder & Rowe is a sham",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.conclusion": "Rosie was in and out of the billiards room from 9.30 until after ten, and Daniel never left it. By the time he stumbled into the study at 10.45, his father had been dead for an hour.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.options.0": "Yes — he was alone in the study at 10.45",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.options.1": "Yes — he slipped out of the billiards room",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.options.2": "No — he was in the billiards room when Lionel was poisoned",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.proofLabels.0": "Where Daniel was",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.proofLabels.1": "When Lionel died",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.question": "Could Daniel have poisoned his father?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_daniel_innocent.title": "Daniel didn’t do it",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.conclusion": "The Vermitox tin in the boot room was opened after the gardener left on Friday. Anyone in the house could have reached it — but Hobbs saw only Eleanor and Daniel pass through.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.options.0": "Dr. Harrow’s medical bag",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.options.1": "The gardener’s tin of rabbit poison in the boot room",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.options.2": "Lionel’s own heart tablets",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.options.3": "It was brought down from London",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.proofLabels.0": "The source",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.proofLabels.1": "A link to the death",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.question": "Where did the cyanide come from?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poison_source.title": "Rabbit poison from the boot room",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.conclusion": "Someone laced Lionel’s whisky with cyanide, tipped the dregs into the fern and polished the glass clean. The temple wound came from his fall.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.options.0": "Struck down with the bronze bookend",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.options.1": "Poisoned — cyanide in his whisky",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.options.2": "A heart attack brought on by the quarrel",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.options.3": "Smothered, then staged as a fall",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.proofLabels.0": "Signs of cyanide",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.proofLabels.1": "What he drank",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.question": "How did Lionel Thorne really die?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_poisoned.title": "Cyanide in the whisky",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.conclusion": "The words Hobbs heard were dictated on 15 November, and that very reel was in the recorder. The ‘voice’ at 10.15 proves nothing about when Lionel died.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.options.0": "Lionel dictating a new letter",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.options.1": "Someone imitating Lionel’s voice",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.options.2": "An old dictation reel playing on the tape recorder",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.options.3": "The wireless from the drawing room",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.proofLabels.0": "What Hobbs heard",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.proofLabels.1": "Why it wasn’t live",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.proofLabels.2": "What played it",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.question": "What did Hobbs really hear through the study door at 10.15?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_recording.title": "The voice was a recording",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.conclusion": "The recorder’s flex was run to the hidden socket behind the desk, through a time switch set for 10.14 to 10.22. Whoever set it bought themselves an alibi at the bridge table — then had to take the switch away after the body was found.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.options.0": "Lionel set it running himself before he died",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.options.1": "A clockwork time switch, wired into the desk lamp’s socket",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.options.2": "Someone slipped into the study at 10.14",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.options.3": "The telephone line set it off",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.proofLabels.0": "The device",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.proofLabels.1": "How it was wired in",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.question": "How did the recorder start playing at 10.14 with Lionel already dead?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_switch.title": "A time switch faked the alibi",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.conclusion": "Hobbs saw him well at 9.25. He never missed his ten o’clock tablet, yet Saturday’s was still in the box. Lionel died between 9.25 and 10.00.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.0": "Between 9.25 and 10.00 pm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.1": "Between 10.15 and 10.45 pm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.2": "Between 10.45 and 11.10 pm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.3": "Before he left the dinner table",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.proofLabels.0": "Last seen alive",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.proofLabels.1": "The tablet he never took",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.proofLabels.2": "His ten o’clock habit",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.question": "When did Lionel actually die?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.title": "Dead before ten o’clock",
 "dialogues:statements.st_daniel_billiards.text": "After dinner I drank in the billiards room. I never set foot in the study.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_daniel_boot_room.text": "After the quarrel I came in from the terrace through the boot room.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_daniel_found_body.text": "At a quarter to eleven Father was already dead in the dark study. I picked up the bookend from the floor and burned my IOU.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_daniel_will.text": "On Monday Father was changing his will to cut me out.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_eleanor_calder.text": "Calder & Rowe is an ordinary supplier of brass fittings.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_eleanor_embezzle.text": "I invented Calder & Rowe and took the money. But I did not kill him.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_eleanor_hall.text": "When the body was found I went to the hall to telephone. The line was dead.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_eleanor_letters.text": "At about twenty to ten I took Mr. Thorne letters to sign. He was perfectly well when I left at a quarter to ten.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_eleanor_reels.text": "I transcribe Mr. Thorne’s dictation each morning and keep the reels on my shelf.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_eleanor_typing.text": "From the end of dinner until ten I was in my office, typing the toast speech.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_finch_eleanor_knees.text": "When we rushed into the study, Eleanor was on her knees behind the desk, then went to telephone.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_finch_library.text": "Between dinner and bridge I was alone in the library with a book.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_finch_phone.text": "From 9.32 to 9.51 I was on the hall telephone to Rupert Hartley.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_finch_saw_eleanor.text": "At about twenty to ten Eleanor went to the study with a folder, and came back ten minutes or so later.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_harrow_blow.text": "A blow to the temple killed him, less than an hour before 11.15.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_harrow_conservatory.text": "I was with Margaret in the conservatory from half past nine until five to ten.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_harrow_cyanide.text": "Lionel was poisoned with cyanide, which kills within minutes. The temple wound happened as he fell.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_harrow_drawing_room.text": "I was in the drawing room all evening.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_harrow_earlier.text": "Medically, Lionel could have been dead since half past nine.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_harrow_pill.text": "Lionel took one digitalis tablet at ten o’clock sharp every night. He never missed.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_hobbs_boot_room.text": "Since Friday only Miss Voss (at four on Saturday) and Mr. Daniel (after the quarrel) came through the boot room.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_hobbs_ice.text": "At 9.25 I took the master fresh ice. He poured his own whisky and was well.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_hobbs_lamp.text": "The master never worked in the dark, and the recorder always plugs in beside the bookcase.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_hobbs_voice.text": "At 10.15 I heard the master dictating: ‘I regret I must decline your offer of the fourteenth.’",
 "dialogues:statements.st_margaret_conservatory.text": "Felix and I were together in the conservatory from half past nine until nearly ten.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_margaret_drawing_room.text": "After dinner I stayed in the drawing room until the alarm.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_margaret_eleanor_hall.text": "After the body was found, Eleanor went out to the hall telephone alone.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_margaret_pill.text": "Lionel took his digitalis at ten o’clock exactly, every night. He never missed in five years.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_margaret_toast.text": "Lionel said the toast would ‘settle old debts and clear the decks’.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_boot_room.lines.0": "After the row I went out on the terrace to cool off. In the rain, like a fool. I came back in through the boot room, dripping, and kicked my shoes off there.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_boot_room.lines.1": "I didn’t touch any rabbit poison, if that’s where this is going. I didn’t even know we kept any.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_boot_room.prompt": "Did you go into the boot room last night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_evening.lines.0": "Where did you go after dinner, Mr. Thorne?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_evening.lines.1": "After Father had finished humiliating me? The billiards room, with a bottle of his best Scotch. I stayed there until Hobbs started shrieking.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_evening.lines.2": "I never set foot in the study after dinner, if that’s what you’re fishing for.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_evening.prompt": "Where did you go after dinner?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_father.lines.0": "Precise. He set every clock in this house himself, every Sunday. He liked things to run to time — and people to run to him.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_father.lines.1": "He trusted exactly two people: Hobbs, and that secretary of his. Certainly not me.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_father.prompt": "What kind of man was your father?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_found.lines.0": "It was dark. The desk lamp was off — just the fire glowing. He was slumped over the desk. I said his name twice.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_found.lines.1": "The tape machine was silent. The room smelled of whisky and coal smoke. That’s all I remember. That, and running.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_found.prompt": "Tell me exactly what you saw in the study.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_quarrel.lines.0": "The whole table heard you threaten him.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_quarrel.lines.1": "He’d bought up my IOU — three thousand pounds’ worth — from a man in Soho. Waved the thing at me over the soup.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_quarrel.lines.2": "On Monday he meant to change his will and cut me out, unless I started at the works as a junior clerk. A clerk! At thirty-two!",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_quarrel.lines.3": "So yes, I said I’d sooner see him in his grave. People say things.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_quarrel.prompt": "What was the quarrel at dinner about?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_suspicion.lines.0": "Gerald, for one. Father had found out something about him and Hartley — he was gloating about it before dinner.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_suspicion.lines.1": "And Eleanor’s been as jumpy as a cat all week. Nineteen years she’s run his life. Ask her what she’s afraid of.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_daniel_suspicion.prompt": "Who do you think wanted your father dead?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_alarm.lines.0": "I went out to the hall to telephone for the police. The line was dead — the storm. Then I came back and sat with Mrs. Thorne.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_alarm.prompt": "What did you do when the body was found?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_calder.lines.0": "A supplier of brass fittings. Perfectly ordinary. I’ve settled their invoices for years.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_calder.lines.1": "Mr. Thorne queried one this week, I believe. He queried everything.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_calder.prompt": "What is Calder & Rowe Supplies?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_duties.lines.0": "Everything. His diary, his letters, the accounts. He dictated onto reels each evening; I transcribed them each morning and filed the reels on my shelf.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_duties.lines.1": "Nineteen years. Some days I knew his mind better than he did.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_duties.prompt": "What did your work for Mr. Thorne involve?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_evening.lines.0": "In my office, typing Mr. Thorne’s speech for the midnight toast. From the end of dinner until ten, when I joined the others for bridge.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_evening.lines.1": "You’ll want to see my office, I expect — Constable Pike has asked for the key twice already. Here. I keep it locked at night; Mr. Thorne’s papers are confidential.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_evening.lines.2": "She hands you a small brass key. The Secretary’s Office is now open to you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_evening.prompt": "Where were you after dinner?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_reel.lines.0": "Then someone took it from my shelf. I don’t lock the office during the day — anyone in the house could have walked in.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_reel.lines.1": "I’d hardly label my own reels and then leave one in the machine for you to find, would I?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_reel.prompt": "The reel in the recorder came from your shelf.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_switch.lines.0": "How ingenious. And how very convenient for whoever was nowhere near the study at a quarter past ten.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_switch.lines.1": "That describes four of us — Mrs. Thorne, Dr. Harrow, Mr. Finch and me. You’ll have to do better than that.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_switch.prompt": "Someone used a time switch to fake Lionel’s voice.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_toast.lines.0": "His retirement, and the name of his successor. He hadn’t told me whom — he meant to write the name in himself.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_toast.lines.1": "Mr. Thorne liked his surprises.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_eleanor_toast.prompt": "What was the midnight announcement?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_alarm.lines.0": "Felix bent over Lionel. Margaret went as white as paper. I stood in the doorway, I’m ashamed to say.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_alarm.lines.1": "Eleanor — now you mention it — was down on her knees behind the desk, by the skirting board. I thought she’d come over faint. Then she stood up, very composed, and said she’d telephone for the police.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_alarm.prompt": "When you all rushed into the study, what did you see?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_company.lines.0": "Lionel and I built it together. We disagreed about where it should go next. Partners do.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_company.lines.1": "Hartley Precision made us an offer this month. Lionel refused it without consulting me. That was Lionel all over.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_company.prompt": "How were things at Thorne Instruments?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_evening.lines.0": "In the library, with a book. Trollope. Alone, I’m afraid — nobody to vouch for me. Then bridge from ten.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_evening.prompt": "Where were you between dinner and bridge?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_note.lines.0": "So he’d found out. He meant to make me resign last night — or announce it for me at his precious midnight toast.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_note.lines.1": "It would have been humiliating. It would not have been worth killing a friend of forty years. I’m sixty; I can bear a little humiliation.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_note.prompt": "Lionel left you a note. He knew about Hartley.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_saw.lines.0": "The telephone table looks straight down the corridor to the study, and across into the conservatory through the glass door.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_saw.lines.1": "Margaret and Felix were in the conservatory — thick as thieves, as they have been for months. And Eleanor came past at about twenty to ten with a folder under her arm, heading for the study.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_saw.lines.2": "She came back ten minutes later, perhaps a little more. Walked right past me without a glance. I assumed Lionel had letters to sign.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_finch_saw.prompt": "From the telephone, what could you see?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_cause.lines.0": "A blow to the left temple. The bronze bookend, I’d say — it was lying right beside him. The poor devil was struck down at his own desk.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_cause.lines.1": "When I examined him at a quarter past eleven he’d been dead under an hour. Hobbs heard him dictating at a quarter past ten, so there’s your window.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_cause.prompt": "How did Lionel die?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_earlier.lines.0": "If it was cyanide — yes. Easily. It kills in minutes, and that room was as warm as an oven from the fire.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_earlier.lines.1": "I took Hobbs’s ‘dictation’ at a quarter past ten as gospel. Medically, Lionel could have been dead since half past nine.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_earlier.prompt": "Could he have died earlier than you thought?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_evening.lines.0": "The drawing room, all evening. Brandy, then bridge from ten o’clock. Margaret and I partnered against Eleanor and Gerald.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_evening.prompt": "Where were you after dinner?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_lionel.lines.0": "Brilliant. Impossible. He built Thorne Instruments out of a shed in Coventry and never forgave anyone for being slower than he was.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_lionel.lines.1": "We were in the desert together in ’42. He saved my life once. I’ve never been allowed to forget it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_lionel.prompt": "What was Lionel like?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_margaret.lines.0": "I’m not proud of it. Lionel was my friend. But I love her — and he loved his company.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_margaret.prompt": "You and Margaret…",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_pills.lines.0": "Digitalis. One tablet at ten o’clock sharp every night, with a glass of water. I made him swear to it after his attack in 1957.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_pills.lines.1": "He never missed. Not once. He was terrified of his heart.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_pills.prompt": "Tell me about Lionel’s heart tablets.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_poisons.lines.0": "Not from my bag, if that’s what you’re thinking — I carry nothing of the kind. But every country house has something. Wasp killer. Rabbit gas.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_poisons.lines.1": "Ask Hobbs what the gardener keeps in the boot room.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_harrow_poisons.prompt": "Where would someone get cyanide in a house like this?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_affair.lines.0": "It isn’t my place to say.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_affair.lines.1": "…I have seen them in the conservatory. More than once. I would not have had the master hurt by it, so I held my tongue.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_affair.prompt": "Did you know about Mrs. Thorne and Dr. Harrow?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_body.lines.0": "At ten past eleven Mrs. Thorne sent me to fetch him for the toast. The room was dark but for the embers. He was slumped over his desk.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_body.lines.1": "Thirty years I’ve served in this house. I never heard myself cry out like that.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_body.prompt": "How did you find him?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_boot_room.lines.0": "That tin was sealed when Jenkins the gardener left on Friday. I checked the stores myself.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_boot_room.lines.1": "Since then? Miss Voss came through for her galoshes at four o’clock yesterday — a walk before the rain, she said. And Mr. Daniel came in that way after the quarrel, soaked to the skin.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_boot_room.prompt": "Who has been in the boot room?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_ice.lines.0": "At twenty-five past nine I took him fresh ice. He poured his own whisky — he always did — and was in fine fettle. Humming, even.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_ice.lines.1": "Then I went down to my supper with Mrs. Bell at half past.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_ice.prompt": "When did you last see Mr. Thorne?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_lamp.lines.0": "Unplugged? The master never sat in the dark. That lamp was always lit when he worked.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_lamp.lines.1": "And the recorder lives on the side table by the bookcase. It plugs in right there beside it. There’d be no call to run the flex across the room.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_lamp.prompt": "The desk lamp was unplugged.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_routine.lines.0": "Whisky at half past nine. His tablet at ten o’clock precisely. The fire banked at a quarter past. He set every clock in the house by the wireless pips on a Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_routine.lines.1": "The master was a man of habits. Everyone who knew him knew them.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_routine.prompt": "What was Mr. Thorne’s evening routine?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_switches.lines.0": "That’s one of ours. We keep three in the hall cupboard, for the lamps when the family’s in London. To deter burglars.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_switches.lines.1": "Anyone in the house would know where they’re kept.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_switches.prompt": "Do you recognise this time switch?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_voice.lines.0": "At a quarter past ten I went up to bank the study fire, as I do every night.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_voice.lines.1": "Through the door I heard the master dictating. Clear as you like: ‘I regret I must decline your offer of the fourteenth.’ So I came away. The master never permitted interruption while he was dictating.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_voice.lines.2": "Everyone in the house knows that rule.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_hobbs_voice.prompt": "Tell me about a quarter past ten last night.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_alarm.lines.0": "Hobbs cried out and we all ran in. Felix went straight to Lionel. Gerald stood in the doorway like a statue.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_alarm.lines.1": "Eleanor said she would telephone for the police and went out to the hall — alone. The line was dead, of course. And I’m afraid I fainted.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_alarm.prompt": "What happened when the body was found?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_daniel.lines.0": "Daniel is weak, not wicked. He’s been in debt since Cambridge.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_daniel.lines.1": "I’ve been paying some of it myself, if you must know. Lionel never knew. Please don’t tell Daniel I told you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_daniel.prompt": "Could Daniel have done this?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_eleanor.lines.0": "Lionel trusted her with everything. The books, the bank, his signature, his diary. More than he ever trusted me.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_eleanor.lines.1": "Nineteen years at his elbow. Some days I think she was more married to him than I was.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_eleanor.prompt": "What do you make of Eleanor Voss?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_evening.lines.0": "In the drawing room, with the others. Coffee, a little brandy, then bridge from ten.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_evening.lines.1": "I didn’t leave it until Hobbs raised the alarm.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_evening.prompt": "Where were you after dinner?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_felix.lines.0": "Two years. Lionel was married to Thorne Instruments long before he married me.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_felix.lines.1": "I’d have asked him for a divorce eventually. I would never have needed to kill him for it. Please — be discreet.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_felix.prompt": "How long have you and Dr. Harrow been…?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_health.lines.0": "His heart was weak. Felix had him on digitalis — one tablet at ten o’clock every night, exactly.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_health.lines.1": "Lionel never missed it. Not once in five years. He was more afraid of his heart than of anything on earth.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_health.prompt": "Was your husband in good health?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_toast.lines.0": "He wouldn’t tell me. He was terribly pleased with himself about it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_toast.lines.1": "He said the toast would ‘settle old debts and clear the decks’. Lionel loved a mystery — as long as he was the one keeping it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_margaret_toast.prompt": "What was Lionel going to announce at midnight?",
 "evidence:evidence.bridge_scorepad.detail": "The score pad from last night’s game: ‘Rubber 1 — 10.00’. Partners M.T. & F.H. against E.V. & G.F. Three rubbers; the last entry is timed 11.05.\n\nFor the hour after ten o’clock, four people gave each other an alibi.",
 "evidence:evidence.bridge_scorepad.name": "Bridge score pad",
 "evidence:evidence.bridge_scorepad.summary": "Bridge from 10.00 to 11.05: Margaret and Harrow against Eleanor and Finch.",
 "evidence:evidence.bronze_bookend.detail": "A heavy bronze horse, one of a pair, tagged by Constable Pike. There is a little blood on one corner. Pike has dusted it and compared the prints, roughly, against the whisky glass Daniel left in the billiards room: ‘His, near as makes no odds.’\n\nFor a fatal blow, the wound it supposedly made was a small one. It barely bled.",
 "evidence:evidence.bronze_bookend.name": "Bronze bookend",
 "evidence:evidence.bronze_bookend.summary": "Blood on one corner — and Daniel’s fingerprints.",
 "evidence:evidence.burnt_iou.detail": "Among the ashes of the study fire, a charred scrap that didn’t quite burn: ‘…promise to pay… the sum of £3,000… Daniel Thorne’.\n\nIt lies on top of the ash, not under it. It was thrown on the fire late in the evening, after the fire had died down.",
 "evidence:evidence.burnt_iou.name": "Burnt IOU",
 "evidence:evidence.burnt_iou.summary": "A charred scrap: ‘…£3,000… Daniel Thorne’.",
 "evidence:evidence.calder_invoice.detail": "An invoice from ‘Calder & Rowe Supplies — Brass Fittings, Birmingham’ for £1,140, marked paid. Lionel has ringed the total in red pencil and added a question mark.\n\nThe typing is slightly uneven: one letter sits high and prints faint wherever it appears.",
 "evidence:evidence.calder_invoice.name": "Calder & Rowe invoice",
 "evidence:evidence.calder_invoice.summary": "Ringed in red by Lionel, with a question mark.",
 "evidence:evidence.carbon_hartley.detail": "A carbon copy dated 15 November, to Sir Rupert Hartley, Hartley Precision Ltd. (telephone WHItehall 4021):\n\n‘Dear Rupert — I regret I must decline your offer of the fourteenth. Thorne Instruments is not for sale — not while I draw breath. — L. Thorne’\n\nTyped by Eleanor from Thursday’s dictation.",
 "evidence:evidence.carbon_hartley.name": "Carbon copy to Hartley",
 "evidence:evidence.carbon_hartley.summary": "15 Nov: ‘I regret I must decline your offer of the fourteenth.’",
 "evidence:evidence.cold_grate.detail": "The fire in Eleanor’s office is laid ready — paper, kindling and coal — but was never lit. The grate is stone cold.\n\nWhatever Eleanor did last night, she didn’t spend a raw November evening in here typing.",
 "evidence:evidence.cold_grate.name": "Cold grate",
 "evidence:evidence.cold_grate.summary": "Eleanor’s office fire was laid, but never lit last night.",
 "evidence:evidence.constable_notes.detail": "Saturday 17 November. Heavy rain from 9 pm.\n\n7.30 pm — Dinner. Present: the deceased, Mrs. Margaret Thorne, Mr. Daniel Thorne, Miss Eleanor Voss, Mr. Gerald Finch, Dr. Felix Harrow. Butler A. Hobbs serving.\n8.45 pm — Quarrel between deceased and son. Son heard to say ‘I’d sooner see you in your grave.’\n9.00 pm — Deceased retires to study. Not to be disturbed until the midnight toast.\n10.00 pm — Bridge in drawing room until 11.05.\n10.15 pm — Butler hears deceased dictating through study door.\n10.45 pm — Housemaid sees son leave the study.\n11.10 pm — Butler finds deceased slumped at desk. Wound to left temple; bronze bookend on floor. Dr. Harrow: dead ‘within the hour’.\n11.15 pm — Telephone line found dead (storm).\n12.30 am — Study sealed by me.\n\n— Const. R. Pike",
 "evidence:evidence.constable_notes.name": "Constable Pike’s notes",
 "evidence:evidence.constable_notes.summary": "The night’s events as the constable has them.",
 "evidence:evidence.cook_statement.detail": "Statement of Mrs. Bell, cook, taken by Const. Pike:\n\n‘Mr. Hobbs came down at half past nine and had his supper with me in the servants’ hall. At a quarter past ten he went up to see to the study fire and was back inside two minutes, saying the master was dictating and not to be disturbed. He never stirred again until Mrs. Thorne rang for him at ten past eleven.’",
 "evidence:evidence.cook_statement.name": "Statement of Mrs. Bell",
 "evidence:evidence.cook_statement.summary": "The cook: Hobbs was in the servants’ hall from 9.30, apart from two minutes at 10.15.",
 "evidence:evidence.desk_diary.detail": "THU 15 — Hartley’s offer: declined (dictated). Calder & Rowe?? No such firm in the trade directory.\n\nSAT 17 — Toast at midnight. Clear the decks.\n\nMON 19 — 10.00 Pemberton & Co.: full audit of the books. 3.00 Osgood: new will.",
 "evidence:evidence.desk_diary.name": "Lionel’s desk diary",
 "evidence:evidence.desk_diary.summary": "Monday: the audit at ten, the new will at three.",
 "evidence:evidence.finch_note.detail": "Folded in the desk drawer, a note in Lionel’s hand, never sent:\n\n‘Gerald — I know about Hartley. Resign quietly before the toast, or I shall announce it for you at midnight. — L.’",
 "evidence:evidence.finch_note.name": "Unsent note to Finch",
 "evidence:evidence.finch_note.summary": "Lionel knew about Finch’s dealings with Hartley.",
 "evidence:evidence.half_signature.detail": "In Eleanor’s out-tray, a folder of Saturday’s letters, typed and signed in Lionel’s hand.\n\nThe last one is signed only halfway — ‘Lionel Th—’ — the ink trailing away in a long, wavering line off the edge of the page.",
 "evidence:evidence.half_signature.name": "Half-signed letter",
 "evidence:evidence.half_signature.summary": "‘Lionel Th—’, the nib trailing off the page.",
 "evidence:evidence.love_note.detail": "Folded small and tucked beneath the embroidery silks in Margaret’s sewing basket:\n\n‘My darling M — the conservatory at half past nine, while they’re all at their brandy. Burn this. — F.’",
 "evidence:evidence.love_note.name": "Note in the sewing basket",
 "evidence:evidence.love_note.summary": "‘The conservatory at half past nine… Burn this. — F.’",
 "evidence:evidence.maid_statement.detail": "Statement of Rosie Dunn, housemaid, taken by Const. Pike:\n\n‘From half past nine I was up and down to the billiards room — Mr. Daniel kept ringing for soda and ice. Until gone ten he never left that room, sir; he was in no fit state to.\n\nAt twenty to ten I looked in at Miss Voss’s office to make up her fire. Nobody there — lamp off, the typewriter under its cover.\n\nAt a quarter to eleven I saw Mr. Daniel come out of the study, white as a sheet.’",
 "evidence:evidence.maid_statement.name": "Statement of Rosie Dunn",
 "evidence:evidence.maid_statement.summary": "The housemaid: Daniel never left the billiards room; Eleanor’s office was empty at 9.40.",
 "evidence:evidence.phone_pad.detail": "The top sheet of the telephone pad has been torn away, but the pencil pressed through. Shading the sheet beneath lightly brings it back:\n\n‘Hartley — WHI 4021 — booked 9.32 — 19 min — 4s 9d — G.F.’",
 "evidence:evidence.phone_pad.name": "Telephone pad impression",
 "evidence:evidence.phone_pad.summary": "A 19-minute call to WHItehall 4021, booked at 9.32 by ‘G.F.’",
 "evidence:evidence.pill_box.detail": "A silver pill box with seven little lids, engraved with the days of the week. Each holds one digitalis tablet.\n\nSunday’s tablet is there — and so is Saturday’s.",
 "evidence:evidence.pill_box.name": "Silver pill box",
 "evidence:evidence.pill_box.summary": "Saturday’s tablet was never taken.",
 "evidence:evidence.rabbit_poison.detail": "On the boot-room shelf: a tin of Vermitox rabbit-gassing powder. CYANIDE — POISON, the label warns.\n\nThe lid has been prised up recently — the scratches are fresh and bright — and the powder inside bears the scoop-mark of a spoon.",
 "evidence:evidence.rabbit_poison.name": "Tin of rabbit poison",
 "evidence:evidence.rabbit_poison.summary": "Vermitox cyanide powder, recently opened.",
 "evidence:evidence.shawl_case.detail": "On the wicker settee: Margaret’s grey cashmere shawl. Beneath a cushion, a silver cigarette case engraved ‘F.H.’\n\nThere are two cigarette ends in the orchid pot, one of them lipsticked.",
 "evidence:evidence.shawl_case.name": "Shawl and cigarette case",
 "evidence:evidence.shawl_case.summary": "Margaret’s shawl, and a case engraved ‘F.H.’, in the conservatory.",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_recorder.detail": "A Grundig reel-to-reel on the side table by the bookcase. The reel is labelled ‘15 NOV — HARTLEY / BANK / MISC’ in a neat hand, and is wound about a third of the way through.\n\nOddly, the flex isn’t plugged into the socket right beside it. It runs across the carpet to the socket behind the desk.",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_recorder.name": "Tape recorder",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_recorder.summary": "Holds a reel labelled ‘15 NOV — HARTLEY’, plugged in across the room.",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_shelf.detail": "Lionel’s dictation reels in labelled boxes, a neat row by date in Eleanor’s hand. One box gapes empty: ‘15 NOV — HARTLEY / BANK / MISC’.\n\nEleanor transcribes the reels each morning. Otherwise, they never leave this shelf.",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_shelf.name": "Shelf of dictation reels",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_shelf.summary": "One box is empty: ‘15 NOV — HARTLEY / BANK / MISC’.",
 "evidence:evidence.time_switch.detail": "Wedged beneath the directory in the telephone-table drawer: a clockwork time switch, the kind used to light lamps while a house stands empty.\n\nIts dial tappets are set: ON at 10.14 pm, OFF at 10.22 pm.",
 "evidence:evidence.time_switch.name": "Clockwork time switch",
 "evidence:evidence.time_switch.summary": "Hidden in the telephone drawer. Set to switch ON at 10.14 and OFF at 10.22.",
 "evidence:evidence.typewriter_flaw.detail": "Eleanor’s Imperial typewriter. A test line still in the machine shows its quirk: every lowercase ‘e’ strikes high and faint.",
 "evidence:evidence.typewriter_flaw.name": "Eleanor’s typewriter",
 "evidence:evidence.typewriter_flaw.summary": "Its lowercase ‘e’ strikes high and faint.",
 "evidence:evidence.unplugged_lamp.detail": "The green-shaded desk lamp is switched on — but dark. Its plug lies on the carpet behind the desk.\n\nThe only socket back there, hidden from the door by the desk, holds the tape recorder’s flex, which has been run all the way across the room.",
 "evidence:evidence.unplugged_lamp.name": "Unplugged desk lamp",
 "evidence:evidence.unplugged_lamp.summary": "Switched on but dark. Its socket holds the recorder’s flex instead.",
 "evidence:evidence.whisky_glass.detail": "A cut-glass tumbler on the desk, polished spotless and bone dry — not so much as a fingerprint on it. Yet the decanter beside it is a good measure down from where Hobbs filled it before dinner.\n\nLionel poured himself a drink. Someone tidied up afterwards.",
 "evidence:evidence.whisky_glass.name": "Polished whisky glass",
 "evidence:evidence.whisky_glass.summary": "Spotless and dry, though a measure is gone from the decanter.",
 "evidence:evidence.wilted_fern.detail": "The potted fern beside the desk has collapsed overnight, its fronds browning at the tips. The soil is damp — someone has poured something into it — and when you lean close it gives off a faint, sweetish smell of bitter almonds.",
 "evidence:evidence.wilted_fern.name": "Wilted fern",
 "evidence:evidence.wilted_fern.summary": "Collapsed overnight; the soil smells of bitter almonds.",
 "suspects:suspects.daniel.bio": "Lionel’s only son, by his first marriage. Charming, idle and three thousand pounds in debt to a London bookmaker. He threatened his father at dinner in front of everyone.",
 "suspects:suspects.daniel.dismissal": "Is that the best you’ve got? I’ve had sterner talks from my tailor.",
 "suspects:suspects.daniel.name": "Daniel Thorne",
 "suspects:suspects.daniel.rebuttal": "Me? Ask Rosie — she was in and out of the billiards room all evening. By the time I went into that study, Father was already gone. You’ve got the wrong Thorne.",
 "suspects:suspects.daniel.role": "The son",
 "suspects:suspects.eleanor.bio": "Lionel’s private secretary for nineteen years. She keeps his diary, his correspondence, his dictation reels and the company’s accounts. Composed, precise, indispensable.",
 "suspects:suspects.eleanor.dismissal": "I’m sure that seemed significant to you.",
 "suspects:suspects.eleanor.name": "Eleanor Voss",
 "suspects:suspects.eleanor.rebuttal": "I was at the bridge table when Hobbs heard Mr. Thorne dictating — with three witnesses. You’ve let your imagination run away with you.",
 "suspects:suspects.eleanor.role": "The secretary",
 "suspects:suspects.finch.bio": "Co-founder and fellow director of Thorne Instruments for thirty years. Shrewd and soft-spoken, and lately at odds with Lionel over the company’s future.",
 "suspects:suspects.finch.dismissal": "I’ve sat across boardroom tables from better bluffers than you.",
 "suspects:suspects.finch.name": "Gerald Finch",
 "suspects:suspects.finch.rebuttal": "I was on the telephone to Rupert Hartley for twenty minutes — the exchange will confirm it. I betrayed Lionel’s company, yes. I didn’t poison him. You want someone who was actually in that room.",
 "suspects:suspects.finch.role": "The partner",
 "suspects:suspects.harrow.bio": "The family’s doctor and Lionel’s friend since the war. He examined the body last night and pronounced on the cause of death.",
 "suspects:suspects.harrow.dismissal": "I’m a doctor, not a fool. That proves nothing at all.",
 "suspects:suspects.harrow.name": "Dr. Felix Harrow",
 "suspects:suspects.harrow.rebuttal": "I was in the conservatory with Margaret, God forgive me — and Gerald saw us there. I’d no more poison Lionel than my own mother. Look to whoever was in that study.",
 "suspects:suspects.harrow.role": "The physician",
 "suspects:suspects.hobbs.bio": "Butler at Larkspur House for thirty years. Discreet, punctual and fiercely loyal. The last person to hear Lionel Thorne alive — or so everyone believes.",
 "suspects:suspects.hobbs.dismissal": "With the greatest respect, I don’t quite follow.",
 "suspects:suspects.hobbs.name": "Albert Hobbs",
 "suspects:suspects.hobbs.rebuttal": "I was in the servants’ hall from half past nine — Mrs. Bell will tell you so. I served Mr. Thorne for thirty years. I’d sooner have cut off my own hand.",
 "suspects:suspects.hobbs.role": "The butler",
 "suspects:suspects.margaret.bio": "Lionel’s second wife of nine years. Elegant, restless and lonely in a house run to the minute. Under the present will she inherits Larkspur House.",
 "suspects:suspects.margaret.dismissal": "I fail to see what that has to do with me.",
 "suspects:suspects.margaret.name": "Margaret Thorne",
 "suspects:suspects.margaret.rebuttal": "How dare you. I was with Felix in the conservatory when it happened — Gerald saw us through the glass. Whatever else I’ve done, I did not kill my husband.",
 "suspects:suspects.margaret.role": "The wife"
}
